[120.418391, "o", "kstraFinder` to verify the end-side meeting behavior with explicit parameters.\\n\\nBut here's the problem—some of these tests might actually expose real bugs. For instance, `BiAStarFinder` seems to return an empty path when start and end are the same node, because marking the end node as opened by the end search (`BY_END=2`) overwrites the start node's opened flag (`BY_START=1`), and then the algorithm fails to recognize the meeting condition properly. I see the issue now—when start equals end, both nodes get marked with different opened values (BY_START then BY_END), but since they're the same node, BY_END overwrites it. On the first iteration, popping startNode from startOpenList closes it, then checking its neighbors won't immediately find a BY_END match since the node itself is already closed. When endNode is then popped from endOpenList, it's already closed too, so the algorithm needs to check if any of its neighbors were marked BY_START to trigger the biBacktrace and return a path.\\n\\nBut wait—if t"]
[120.418434, "o", "he test expects a non-empty path and the algorithm is returning an empty array, that test should be failing, yet the test results showed 294 passing tests. Let me look more carefully at what the test actually expects versus what the code would return.\\n\\nActually, I think I've been misunderstanding the state transitions. When startNode gets its `opened` property set twice—first to `BY_START`, then to `BY_END`—the second assignment overwrites the first. So in the first iteration, when I pop startNode from the startOpenList and mark it closed, then try to pop it from the endOpenList, it's already been closed. But more importantly, I need to trace through whether the algorithm actually hits the biBacktrace condition or if there's something about the neighbor expansion I'm missing. wait: in the while loop, BOTH pops happen in one iteration. First from startOpenList, then from endOpenList. So in a single loop iteration:\\n  1. Pop startNode from startOpenList → closed = true\\n  2. Expand its neighbors (none a"]
