[210.849153, "o", " a node already in the open list. Let me think of a grid where this actually happens...\\n\\nWith uniform costs, I can construct a scenario where a node gets added with a higher g-value first, then discovered again with a lower g-value later. Rather than trying to engineer the exact conditions, I'll write a test that exercises this scenario and verify the pathfinding still works correctly.\\n\\nBut I should really prioritize the critical missing tests first—the start==end cases for BiBestFirstFinder and IDAStarFinder are blocking, then move through the priority 2 tests like the diagonal variants and the recursive tracking behavior.\\n\\nNow I'm checking whether BiDijkstraFinder actually has explicit coverage for end-side meeting. The existing \\\"path is contiguous\\\" test on a 9x1 grid does exercise this indirectly since BiDijkstra inherits the bidirectional logic, but there's no test explicitly documenting that the end-side expansion finds and meets the start-side node. I'll add a dedicated test for that scenario."]
