[332.962504, "o", "{\"type\":\"assistant\",\"message\":{\"model\":\"claude-sonnet-4-6\",\"id\":\"msg_01PAJ2bTmguf5pZEfP1sH2AA\",\"type\":\"message\",\"role\":\"assistant\",\"content\":[{\"type\":\"thinking\",\"thinking\":\"Before my changes: 377 passing, 3 failing.\\nAfter my changes: 392 passing, 2 failing.\\n\\nSo my changes:\\n- Added 15 new tests (392 - 377 = 15 new passing tests)\\n- Fixed 1 pre-existing failing test (3 - 2 = 1 failure fixed)\\n- No regressions (still 2 pre-existing failures in JPF tests)\\n\\nThat's great! The 2 remaining failures are JPF tests that were pre-existing (different workers' territory). My changes actually improved things.\\n\\n\\nLet me verify this: the 3 failures before were the 2 JPF tests plus 1 other failure that my trackRecursion fix resolved, leaving just the 2 JPF failures now.\\n\\nActually, I need to reconsider what happened with git stash—it only stashes tracked files, so the untracked test files that other workers added wouldn't have been stashed and would still be running. Let me look more carefully at which test files ar"]
